* Dogfood debugger task 2026-07-18: hand-written 68000 assembly OS-9
* module carrying a deliberate, non-obvious logic bug, found and fixed
* using the real OS-9 `debug` command live on os9exec (via
* tools/os9repl.sh) -- not by re-reading the source. This exercises
* `common/using-os9exec-repl.md`'s "Symbolic debugging (the OS-9 `debug`
* command)" section, mirroring an earlier same-day 6809 dogfood pass
* that found a real register-clobbering bug purely through breakpoints
* and register inspection (see 6809-live-verification/'s equivalent
* report) -- this is the 68k side of that exercise.
*
* THE PROGRAM: sums ten signed-byte "delta" values (deltas below) into a
* 32-bit accumulator (d1), and separately counts how many of them are
* negative (d6), then prints both totals via I$Write. Correct answer for
* the fixed data set {5,-3,8,-12,20,-1,15,-7,3,-20}: Sum=8, Negatives=5.
*
* THE BUG (now fixed -- see the `notneg:` block below for the corrected
* code, and the comment there for exactly what was wrong): each byte was
* meant to be sign-extended to a long before being added to the running
* sum (`ext.w d0` then `ext.l d0`). The buggy version instead read
* `and.w #$00FF,d0` in place of `ext.w d0` -- a very natural-looking
* "isolate/widen the byte" idiom that is simply wrong for *signed* data:
* it zero-extends instead of sign-extends, and the following `ext.l d0`
* becomes a no-op once `and.w` has already forced bit 15 to 0. Every
* negative byte (top bit set) is silently reinterpreted as a large
* *positive* value (e.g. -3 -> +253) instead of staying negative. The
* separate negative-count logic (`tst.b d0` / `bpl.s notneg`, which runs
* *before* the widening) is unaffected, so `Negatives:` prints correctly
* (5) while `Sum:` is wrong (1288 instead of 8) -- a real "one stat right,
* one wrong" signal, not an obviously-broken program.
*
* Buggy output (verified live, `/dd/USR/CLAUDE/sumstats` before the fix):
*   Sum:        1288
*   Negatives:  5
* Fixed output (verified live, after the one-line fix below):
*   Sum:        8
*   Negatives:  5
*
* THE DEBUG SESSION (all live on os9exec via tools/os9repl.sh, full
* speed with `-r`, logged in as the `claude` account):
*   1. `r68 -O=<name>.r <name>.a` to assemble, `l68 -g -O=<name> <name>.r`
*      to link with debugger symbol data, then `debug <name>` to enter.
*   2. First attempt used plain (colon-less) labels -- `debug`'s `sc`
*      command then showed only three symbols (`btext`/`bname`/`etext`,
*      the module's own boundary markers) with none of this program's
*      own labels (`start`/`sumloop`/`notneg`/`printnum`/...) visible at
*      all, and `l68 -s`'s linkage-map listing confirmed the same three
*      names -- the assembler had not retained the plain labels as
*      exported symbols the linker/debugger could see.
*      `68k/os9-68k-assembly.md`'s "Known gaps" section flags exactly
*      this, unresolved: *"The external-symbol 'trailing colon = public'
*      visibility convention"* (untested). Adding a trailing colon to
*      `start`, `sumloop`, `notneg`, and `printnum` (`start:` etc.) and
*      reassembling/relinking made all four appear correctly in both
*      `l68 -s`'s map and `debug`'s `sc` listing, at their real code
*      addresses -- **this resolves that skill gap: colon-suffixed
*      labels are exported/public on this `r68`/`l68`, colon-less labels
*      are not linker/debugger-visible.** (Not fixed in the skill file
*      by this task per instructions -- diagnostic pass only, flagged
*      for the skill maintainer in the dogfood report.)
*   3. `b sumloop` and `b notneg` (the latter is reached every loop
*      iteration regardless of sign, right before the buggy widening
*      code -- a natural per-iteration checkpoint), then `g` to run to
*      each hit. `debug` auto-prints all registers (`dn:`/`an:`/`pc:`/
*      `cc:`) on every stop, so the documented bare `.` command (also
*      exercised once explicitly) is largely redundant with that.
*   4. `gs` (documented as "run to fall-through", not true single-step)
*      used only for genuinely straight-line runs between breakpoints
*      (stepping over `move.b`/`tst.b`/`and.w`/`ext.l`/`add.l` one at a
*      time) -- never across the loop's own conditional branches, per
*      the skill's explicit warning that `gs` on a taken branch can run
*      an entire extra loop iteration before landing back on the
*      fall-through address by accident. Using `b`+`g` to bracket each
*      branch and `gs` only inside the straight-line stretches worked
*      cleanly and never needed a Ctrl-C recovery.
*   5. Concretely: at the `notneg` breakpoint for the delta -3 (second
*      array element), the register dump showed d0=$000000FD (253) and
*      d1=$00000005 (5, the correct running sum after processing +5).
*      Stepping `gs` through `and.w #$00FF,d0` left d0 unchanged at
*      $000000FD; stepping through `ext.l d0` *also* left it unchanged
*      (already proof the extend was a no-op, since a real sign-extend
*      of a byte whose top bit is set must produce a value with 1s
*      filling the upper bytes, i.e. $FFFFFFFD, not $000000FD); the
*      following `add.l d0,d1` then produced d1=$00000102 (258 = 5+253)
*      instead of the correct d1=$00000002 (5-3=2) -- the exact moment
*      and exact instruction the bug lives in, pinned down by watching
*      the register transcript rather than by reasoning about the code.
*   6. `di sumloop 6` (disassemble without executing) cross-checked the
*      six-instruction loop head against source and confirmed the fix
*      target precisely; `sc` (bare, no argument -- an argument form is
*      separately documented as broken) listed all real code symbols
*      cleanly once the colon fix above was applied.
*
* Assembled with `r68`, linked with `l68 -g`, run/debugged live on real
* os9exec (OS-9/68000) via tools/os9repl.sh, from the `claude` account's
* /dd/USR/CLAUDE (== host os9/h0/USR/CLAUDE, host-native, so authored on
* the host and converted with `flip -m` per using-os9exec-repl.md).
* Module header fields hand-defined as in dogfood-asm-line-counter.a
* (no usable `use`-able I$/F$ call-code defs file found anywhere on this
* disk image -- same finding as that file, not re-derived here).
*
* See dogfood-report-debugger-2026-07-18.md for the full account of
* which debugger commands were used, what matched the skill and what
* didn't.
*
Prgrm  set 1
Objct  set 1
ReEnt  set $80
Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+Objct
Attr_Rev  set (ReEnt<<8)+1
IWrite set $8A
FExit  set $06
*
 psect sumstats,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,400,start
*
* Entry: no command-line arguments used -- this program always operates
* on the fixed `deltas` array below.
start:
 lea     deltas(pc),a0
 moveq   #10,d7           d7 = loop counter (10 values)
 moveq   #0,d1              d1 = running sum (long)
 moveq   #0,d6                d6 = count of negative values
sumloop:
 move.b  (a0)+,d0            d0.b = next delta byte
 tst.b   d0
 bpl.s   notneg
 addq.l  #1,d6
notneg:
 ext.w   d0                    sign-extend byte to word (FIXED: was
*                               `and.w #$00FF,d0`, which zero-extends
*                               instead of sign-extending -- see header
*                               comment for the live debug session that
*                               found this. That version left every
*                               negative byte reinterpreted as a large
*                               positive value, e.g. -3 read back as
*                               +253, because AND cannot restore a sign
*                               bit that it just masked away, and the
*                               following `ext.l` becomes a no-op once
*                               bit 15 is already forced to 0.)
 ext.l   d0                    sign-extend word to long
 add.l   d0,d1
 subq.l  #1,d7
 bne.s   sumloop
*
 move.l  d1,d5              d5 = final sum (save before d1 gets reused for I/O)
*
 lea     summsg(pc),a0
 move.l  #summsglen,d1
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 move.l  d5,d1
 bsr     printnum
*
 lea     negmsg(pc),a0
 move.l  #negmsglen,d1
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 move.l  d6,d1
 bsr     printnum
*
 moveq   #0,d1
 trap    #0
 dc.w    FExit
*
* printnum: d1.l = unsigned value to print as decimal, followed by CR+LF,
* to stdout (path 1).  Self-contained 16-byte stack scratch buffer.
* Clobbers d0-d3,a0,a1.  (Copied verbatim from
* dogfood-asm-line-counter.a's already-live-verified routine.)
printnum:
 sub.l   #16,a7
 movea.l a7,a1
 adda.l  #16,a1
 move.l  d1,d2
 moveq   #0,d3
 tst.l   d2
 bne.s   pn_loop
 move.b  #'0',-(a1)
 moveq   #1,d3
 bra.s   pn_output
pn_loop:
 tst.l   d2
 beq.s   pn_output
 divu.w  #10,d2
 move.w  d2,d0
 swap    d2
 move.b  d2,d1
 add.b   #'0',d1
 move.b  d1,-(a1)
 addq.w  #1,d3
 clr.l   d2
 move.w  d0,d2
 bra.s   pn_loop
pn_output
 movea.l a1,a0
 move.l  d3,d1
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 add.l   #16,a7
 lea     crbyte(pc),a0
 moveq   #2,d1
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 rts
*
deltas   dc.b 5,-3,8,-12,20,-1,15,-7,3,-20
summsg   dc.b "Sum:        "
summsglen equ *-summsg
negmsg   dc.b "Negatives:  "
negmsglen equ *-negmsg
crbyte   dc.b 13,10
*
 ends
